<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wen of Western Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/507">507</a>–551</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Yuan Baoju</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, Yuan Baoju, was installed as emperor in 535 by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> after the poisoning of Emperor Xiaowu, becoming the first ruler of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Wei dynasty</a>. Real power lay with Yuwen Tai throughout his reign, which ended with his death in 551.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuan Baoju, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba clan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowen-of-northern-wei">Emperor Xiaowen of Northern Wei</a>. His father, Yuan Yu, Prince of Jingzhao, rebelled in 508 after being punished for corruption and was killed; his mother, the concubine Yang Aofei, was executed after giving birth, and the boy grew up under arrest with his brothers at Zongzheng Temple until 515. After a plot with Emperor Xiaoming against the lovers of Empress Dowager Hu was discovered he lost his office. He married Lady Yifu in 525, was made Marquess of Shao County in 528 and Prince of Nanyang in 530. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Book of Wei</a>, written by the Eastern Wei official Wei Shou, calls his youth dissolute, a judgement historians treat with suspicion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 534 he followed his cousin Emperor Xiaowu from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> poisoned Xiaowu, Yuan Shun, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyang">Puyang</a>, persuaded Yuwen to choose the 27-year-old Yuan Baoju over a younger candidate. Yuwen deferred to him in public while holding power himself, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>, then weaker than Eastern Wei, withstood repeated attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a>. In 538 the emperor divorced Empress Yifu at Yuwen's request to marry a daughter of the Rouran khan Yujiulü Anagui. That year, while he and Yuwen went to defend Luoyang, captured Eastern Wei troops briefly seized Chang'an.</p><p>When the Rouran attacked in spring 540, many officials blamed the new empress, and the emperor ordered the former Empress Yifu, whom he had hoped to bring back, to commit suicide; the Rouran empress died in childbirth soon after. In 549 an edict, probably at Yuwen's request, restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> names that Emperor Xiaowen had changed to Han ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> ended Eastern Wei and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a> in 550, Emperor Wen was the only remaining claimant to the Northern Wei throne, but Yuwen's attack on the new state lost several provinces. The emperor died in March 551 and was succeeded by his son Yuan Qin, Emperor Fei; some scholars have speculated that he was poisoned by Hu Zhi, an associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> in charge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">imperial diet</a>. Empress Yifu was eventually buried with him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Wen of Western Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Northern Wei emperor Xiaowu fled to him in 534, Yuwen Tai received him, then had him poisoned and in 535 installed <b>Yuan Baoju</b> as Emperor Wen of Western Wei.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-wei">Western Wei</a></li></ul></section>
